we have new job numbers and they're  they're pretty ugly quite frankly so  before the start of the corona virus  pandemic the u.s. had about a hundred  and fifty eight million people working  now you probably have heard Republicans  bragging about how more people were  working before the corona virus pandemic  than ever before and that's true but  also more people were not working before  the corona virus pandemic than ever  before this is a very popular political  football that I want to remind you of  before we move on to the data which is  the u.s. population grows so if you have  roughly the same portion of the  population working and not working at  almost any point in time you can  accurately say we have more people than  ever working or you can accurately say  we have more people than ever not  working both are often true because the  population keeps getting bigger when  it's a Democratic president the  Republicans focus on the number of  people not working when it's a  Republican president they focus on the  number of people working it's a  meaningless statistic absent population  growth of sort of rates and that is  where that talking point comes from but  needless to say a hundred and fifty  eight million people were working prior  to the start of the corona virus  pandemic we now have a total of at least  twenty two point three million people  who have lost their jobs today we  learned that another five and a quarter  million people filed for unemployment  benefits last week we get the data on a  one-week delay so today we learned about  the numbers from last week this brings  the total to about 21 million people who  have filed for unemployment benefits  since the start of the corona virus  pandemic this is 13 percent of the  working population that have lost their  jobs and the the real number is even  higher and what I mean by that is there  are systems at the state level for  filing for unemployment benefits that  are over the max they are stretched they  are overwhelmed phone systems where it's  just busy they can't even put you in the  hold queue web systems that are just  crashing because of the number of people  trying to file benefits so this is the  number of people that have been able to  file benefit  you've got all the people who have tried  but failed you also have people working  in under the table jobs who do not  qualify for an employment unemployment  benefits traditionally even if  exceptions have been made so that they  do in this particular case many of them  don't know it and they've simply lost  their jobs and not even filed so the  real rate is much higher now we have a  situation where the unemployment rate as  we typically see it this is called the  u3 rate it's going to be almost useless  very soon and I'll explain why the u3 is  going to go up because tens of millions  of people have lost their jobs but it's  likely to go up only a fraction of the  sort of real unemployment rate for this  reason and it's counterintuitive you  might think if thirteen percent of the  workforce lost its job how is it that  the unemployment rate might only go from  like 4 to 6 or 4 to 7 percent it doesn't  make sense the way our system works the  u3 which is that rate that is publicized  though it's 3.8 it's 4.2 when you aren't  looking for work you are no longer  considered unemployed from the purposes  of the unemployment rate this has always  been the case and you know during Obama  as usual as the unemployment rate went  down you had Republicans say the system  is bogus if you stop looking for work  you're no longer considered unemployed  that's been the case for decades so  again it's another distraction technique  that the right loves to use but in this  particular very special situation there  are going to be millions of people who  have just lost their jobs who are not  looking for work because normally when  you file for benefits you have a  questionnaire to fill out or you might  be doing it online you might be  answering it on the phone and you are  asked are you looking for work have you  interviewed anywhere for work give us a  list of where you have and you need to  do that in order to get benefits if you  don't say that you're doing that you  don't get the benefits because so many  of the current job losses are pandemic  related job losses that is not required  you are not required to be looking for  work you're only required to be keeping  in touch with your employer so this  could create a very confusing situation  where despite 22 million people  21 million people losing their jobs we  may only see a couple point uptick in  the official unemployment rate because  that's the way the unemployment rate is  calculated Republicans would complain  about this during Obama's term they'd  say the economy is much worse than the  rate tells you because all these people  are so sick of looking for work in this  terrible Obama economy which wasn't true  that they've stopped looking and so  really the the the u3 is inaccurately  low that's true but that's always the  case here this really matters because  you are not required to be looking for  work to receive unemployment benefits  and then there's the issue of the real  number is way higher over whelmed  unemployment systems as I mentioned  people trying to file claims but unable  to people who have lost jobs where they  traditionally don't get unemployment  benefits so the true number sadly is way  higher than 21 million people now in  real terms how many of these 21 million  jobs are going to reappear as the  pandemic starts to fade the answer is we  don't know number one when we talk about  the pandemic fading there's not going to  be one day where we flip a switch it's  going to be a process it's a process  that could take up to 18 months in some  ways shapes or forms one report said for  restaurants for example July is going to  be the limit for a lot of restaurants at  which point many restaurant owners will  just start walking away from their  leases and not planning to reopen really  really sad because yesterday was the  record death rate a number of deaths  rather in both the United States and  globally so we are not exactly at a time  where we should be planning to reopen  despite Donald Trump announcing  yesterday that today he would give a  list of states that he wants to see  reopen right away one other data point  just out of a curiosity we did a survey  of our own audience and we asked has  your income decreased due to coronavirus  almost 2/3 of our audience says they are  their income is the same or higher  during the virus 11% saw their income  decrease anywhere from 10 to 30 percent  nine percent said their income is down  40 to 70 percent three percent said  their income is down 80 to 90 percent  but 14% of our viewers and listeners say  their income dropped to zero notice that  14% of our audience has lost all their  income  it looks like 13% of the workforce  nationally has gone on unemployment  benefits so very similar numbers numbers  tracking very similarly there so we've  got a big uphill battle to get back to  where we were  industries may change discussions of  nationalizing certain supply chains are  already starting whether it's masks or  medical equipment or whatever because of  the virus anybody pretending to know  what the economic landscape is going to  look like in 18 months is simply  guessing and we need to understand that  we need to sit with the uncertainty  because we really don't have another  choice there's a YouTube channel called  up flip that we're partnering with I  would love for you to check it out I've  put the link right underneath the video  make sure to subscribe to the channel  when you're there up flip is a channel  that is all about exploring small  business and what makes a small business  successful every episode interviews an  entrepreneur you take a tour of the  business and these are not generic  interviews you actually get a really  fascinating deep dive into the history  of the business the mistakes the  entrepreneur made what they learned what  motivates them what a day is like  running their business and you get all  sorts of super interesting and valuable  insight about what it's like to run that  type of small business as a business  owner myself I find up flips videos to  be endlessly intriguing even if you  don't own your own business it is really  great for a general audience it's stuff  that I think just about anybody would  find fascinating and you can check it  out by going to david pakman.com slash  up flip the link is right underneath  this video when you're there make sure  to subscribe and click the bell for  notifications
 

